# Repository Pattern

## Structure

```
Modules/{Name}/app/Repositories/Contracts/{Entity}RepositoryInterface.php
Modules/{Name}/app/Repositories/Eloquent/{Entity}Repository.php
```

Bind interfaces in the module `ServiceProvider`:

```php
$this->app->bind(ProductRepositoryInterface::class, ProductRepository::class);
```

## Repository responsibilities

- Encapsulate Eloquent queries and persistence.
- Expose intention-revealing methods: `findActiveById`, `decrementStock`, `lockForUpdate`.
- Return models/collections/primitives — not HTTP responses.
- For concurrency (checkout): use `lockForUpdate()` inside a DB transaction owned by the Service.

## Service responsibilities

- Orchestrate repositories + domain rules.
- Own `DB::transaction()` for checkout/cancel.
- Throw domain exceptions (e.g. `InsufficientStockException`) — Controllers map them to HTTP.

## Rules

- Never inject Eloquent Models into Controllers for querying.
- Never put SQL/query builder chains in Controllers or FormRequests.
- Prefer interfaces in Service constructors for testability.
- Keep repositories free of discount/checkout business rules.

```php
// ❌ BAD
class CheckoutService {
    public function checkout($user) {
        Product::where('id', $id)->update(['stock' => ...]);
    }
}

// ✅ GOOD
class CheckoutService {
    public function __construct(private ProductRepositoryInterface $products) {}
    public function checkout($user) {
        return DB::transaction(function () use ($user) {
            $product = $this->products->lockForUpdate($id);
            $this->products->decrementStock($product, $qty);
        });
    }
}
```
